  fool's gold  if he was a price hawk in 1973  if he hadn't pushed up the price of oil  perhaps we would not have had this huge  influx of money into his country that so  destabilized the society  that it led to the eruption that that  caused the toppled the peacock throne  perhaps his son would be sitting on that  throne today so i think his pushing up  the price  and the hubris and arrogance that went  with it  did himself in  i think in general  we've had a pretty good deal  in this country in the post-wars years  of suburbanization of oil we've had for  the most part readily available oil and  a couple times when we've had  disruptions it's been cheap  i mean you know  no one likes the price that goes up  anytime the price goes up in los angeles  my mother calls me and you know and  complains  you know and uh and my wife does the  same thing i mean everybody does but  when you look in terms of inflation and  so forth and other things  we've had a pretty good deal we've been  able to get on with our lives and go  where we want when we want  but i don't think that we should think  that anybody  has done this out of a favor for us or  because they like us i think that  nations pursue their interests and  politicians do and sometimes people  suffer from an illness that was first  diagnosed in the 1870s called oil on the  brain when they think  that oil gives unlimited power it gives  great power  but it also  can be  quite  damaging will it be our downfall  i think it's a form of  weakness and what i see in the question  that i focus on  looking at the rest of this decade in  the aftermath of this crisis is how do  we as a country  resolve these environmental values that  have become such a strong part  of our culture  that you know what i always think of we  had an earth day last april and somebody  was 25 day 25 years old at earth day in  1990 was five years old in 1970 they  grew up  with uh with earth day  and environmental values and i think  there's a real gulf  between people one generation another  understanding their respective values  but these values are very strong  and at the same time we do have energy  requirements  that  that power our civilization we have  become a lot more energy efficient i  just did the numbers looked at them  again before i came on this show we're  about 25 percent more energy efficient  today than we were in 1975  but we are going to face these tough  choices  how do we produce do we want to be a  nation that increasingly imports our  energy or not uh do  how do we  safeguard the environmental values that  are so strong  and i don't and i really end the book on  a question mark because i see  that we do it through guerrilla warfare  and the political process and endless  litigation and i just keep thinking  there must be some way we can get a  balance on these issues  a national  consensus as to uh how we want to what  kind of energy future we want as well as  what kind of environmental future one of  the things you do is spend a lot of time  on previous wars and individuals who got  us into war  and let me read you one cut line on the  picture uh adolf hitler made oil central  to his plans for conquest in world war  ii  his ill-conceived invasion of the soviet  union was halted just short of the rich  oil resources  of the caucus  hitler is an interesting case early on  even before i started the book i came  across a line in a book  written  by a german saying that and written in  the 1930s saying that hitler had learned  the lessons from general ludendorff who  was one of the two commander the  two commanders of the german army the  first world war  about  how  the lack of oil at the end of the first  world war had been one of the main  factors that led to germany's surrender  and hitler and i quote some of the  people who worked with him was obsessed  with oil he knew the history of all the  oil fields  he knew their production rates  in the middle of uh  in the aftermath of the battle  stalingrad in the middle of the night at  four am the morning calls general von  monstein his commander in the eastern  front and gives him a lecture about the  significance of oil in world politics  and screams about you know my quarter my  generals don't understand anything about  economics  he was very obsessed with oil and i  begin that chapter  talking about how ig farben came to him  and the beginning of hitler's interest  in the synthetic fuels  which  making oil from coal in which hitler  really committed himself to in 1936 as  part of his girding for war  and  in attacking the soviet union he was  motivated by his hatred of the slavs his  hatred of communism but his two  objectives one was moscow and the other  were those oil fields around baku where  just recently we've seen by ethnic  civil war  and  he was driving for that  in the  north africa general ramo basically fell  apart his campaign because he ran out of  oil he wrote to his wife shortage of  petrol it's enough to make one weep  and  when  so hitler was very focused on this as a  question because you  you couldn't have motorized warfare and  that's what rommel said you couldn't  it's all meaningless  and in 1944  in may  the allied air forces finally began to  attack the german synthetic fuel plants  which were providing about half of  germany's total fuel supply  and  all of its aviation fuel  that day albert shapiro the overlord of  the german war economy later said he  said on that day the technological war  was decided  on that day the allied generals had  finally stopped committing as he put it  absurdities  and that was a critical moment  and  that was the germans ultimately couldn't  get their airplanes into the air even  though they developed jets which would  have been formidable  weapons against us they didn't have the  fuel to fly them  so  uh in some ways you can say that the  circum world war  some ways not by any by all means not it  was what was an oil war oil was  important to 